  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Silky parchment is a thin, shelf-like fungus belonging to the Stereaceae family, characterized by its delicate, papery texture and distinctive striped appearance. It is a wood-decomposing species commonly found on dead hardwood branches throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Silky, striated surface",
      "description": "The upper surface displays fine radial striations or grooves giving it a silky, parchment-like appearance",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin, flexible fruiting body",
      "description": "The shelf is remarkably thin and papery, often less than 1mm thick, and bends without breaking",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smooth to slightly wrinkled underside",
      "description": "The undersurface is smooth or finely wrinkled, lacking gills or pores, and ranges from white to pale cream colored",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Silky parchment grows on dead or dying hardwood branches and logs, particularly favoring exposed wood in moist, shaded forest environments throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike many shelf fungi, silky parchment is so thin and delicate that it can be peeled away from wood almost like fabric, yet it still effectively breaks down tough woody cellulose.",
